WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.501
-Our next guest is
an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:03.701
comedian and writer.

00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:06.467
He's performing
his one-man show,

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:08.634
"The Movement You Need,"
through November 7th

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:09.834
at the SoHo Playhouse.

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:10.968
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:12.868
our very good friend
Brendan Hunt, everybody.

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:14.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:29.367
♪♪

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.434
Hi, my friend.
-Hi, buddy.

00:00:31.434 --> 00:00:33.701
-It's very nice to see you
the day after Halloween.

00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:35.667
Because I've known you
for 20 years.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:38.501
You have made some of the bolder
sartorial choices

00:00:38.501 --> 00:00:40.734
over the years,
and so I was very excited

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:42.701
that you were going to get
to spend Halloween in New York

00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:44.801
and very curious as to
what you would dress as,

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:48.901
and, as often,
it was super confusing.

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:51.234
-Yeah, yeah,
I have a knack for, like,

00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:54.601
combo-platter Halloween costumes
that never work out.

00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:56.934
One year I was --
I went through three stages

00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:59.067
on, like, a party bus
where I was dressed as Neo.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:00.634
-Yeah.
-Then later I added

00:01:00.634 --> 00:01:04.067
a Super Mario hat and mustache
and I was Mar-Neo.

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:05.968
-Yeah.
-And then later,

00:01:05.968 --> 00:01:09.367
most unsuccessfully,
I added a long, curly black wig,

00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:11.167
and I was Mar-Neo Speedwagon.

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:13.868
-Yeah.
-No. No, no.

00:01:13.868 --> 00:01:16.601
-If somebody had come up to you
and gone, "Mar-Neo Speedwagon,"

00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:19.868
you would have known
you were in the Matrix.

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:21.434
This is all simulation.

00:01:21.434 --> 00:01:25.367
Alright, so this is
the Arsenal goalkeeper,

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:27.734
who also plays
for the U.S. national team.

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:29.567
-Yes.
-So he dressed

00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:32.067
like your television character
last year.

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:33.901
-Yes.
-And his name is Matt?

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:35.200
-Matt Turner.
-Matt Turner.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.300
-Now at Nottingham Forest.
-Okay, so now Nottingham.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.334
So Matt Turner was your --

00:01:38.334 --> 00:01:39.868
'cause you were gonna
repay the favor.

00:01:39.868 --> 00:01:41.334
He dressed like you.
You were gonna dress like him.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:42.701
-Yes.
-But that's too easy.

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:45.000
-I didn't want to go
straight Matt Turner.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.601
So I decided I would add
a little something,

00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:49.934
and I went with Bat Turner.

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:52.467
-Bat Turner.

00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:56.567
So...anybody yell out,
"Oh, Bat Turner"?

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:58.868
-No. In fact, I only got
to go to one party,

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:00.501
and the people there --
Like, I was, like,

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:02.300
you know, sat down, had dinner,
had the mask off.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.200
And finally, "Yeah,
so I'm Bat Turner,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.100
and Matt Turner
is a goalkeeper."

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.868
"Oh, thank you.
God. Okay, great."

00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:09.067
-But then you didn't let it go.

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:10.934
You were like, "I'm gonna
amp it up one more time."

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:12.534
-Well, the opportunity arose.

00:02:12.534 --> 00:02:13.968
-So you were...

00:02:13.968 --> 00:02:16.634
-Bat Ternie.
-Okay.

00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:19.601
The best thing here is, like,
you clearly --

00:02:19.601 --> 00:02:24.000
You're just in
a different part of the kitchen.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.467
And, by the way,
I want to point out,

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:28.200
there's no other people
at this party.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.767
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:32.834
Hey, congratulations
on the show.

00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:34.200
I've always, you know --

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.133
I think a lot of people here
know you from television.

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:39.834
I got to know you onstage.
I love watching you onstage.

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:41.234
This is not your first
one-man show,

00:02:41.234 --> 00:02:43.467
but this is probably
the most personal one-man show.

00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:45.601
-Yeah, yeah,
it's basically looking at

00:02:45.601 --> 00:02:48.901
my mom and I's relationship,
which was complicated,

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:51.367
like so many
mom relationships are.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:54.601
But we had, like,
one avenue of clarity

00:02:54.601 --> 00:02:56.267
and, you know, no conflict,

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:58.067
which was our love
of The Beatles,

00:02:58.067 --> 00:03:01.400
which she had engendered in me
and she was 100% right about.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.300
-And so you guys
always had that.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.534
Even when times were tough,
you connected on The Beatles.

00:03:05.534 --> 00:03:09.100
You were a big Beatles fan even,
I mean, as a kid

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:10.434
because of your mom.

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:13.133
So tell me why
you would sign "The Beatles"

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:14.901
in other people's yearbooks.

00:03:14.901 --> 00:03:18.033
-Yeah, so,
in my yearbook picture,

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:20.968
I'm wearing
a pin of The Beatles.

00:03:20.968 --> 00:03:24.701
It's the Beatles butcher cover.
Google it. It's great.

00:03:24.701 --> 00:03:27.367
And somehow, some way,
Kenwood Academy,

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:31.400
2,200-person student body,
not a single other music act

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.167
is represented
in the entire yearbook.

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:35.667
Not a T-shirt, not a poster.
The Beatles.

00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:37.400
And so I was so proud of this

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.801
that when I would sign
people's yearbooks,

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:42.100
I would go to my picture
and I would write "The Beatles"

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.400
just to make extra sure
they knew that The Beatles

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.801
were in their yearbook.

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:48.834
-The weird thing is,
you look more

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:51.167
like young Henry Winkler
than he did.

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:53.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:57.567
Is this a friend
that wrote this?

00:03:57.567 --> 00:04:01.767
-Yeah, so all that Beatles stuff
didn't make me popular.

00:04:01.767 --> 00:04:03.067
Eventually,
one of my best friends

00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:05.801
decided to deface
my own yearbook with,

00:04:05.801 --> 00:04:08.167
"Buttcrust Beatles Suck Fan."

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:09.534
And to his credit,

00:04:09.534 --> 00:04:12.200
I've never heard the word
"buttcrust" before or since.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.567
-Yeah, no.
It's a real --

00:04:14.567 --> 00:04:18.000
Try it once and then
abandon it forever.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.801
You talk about
one of your first --

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:22.968
or a memory that I guess
has stuck over the years

00:04:22.968 --> 00:04:26.133
is your mom introducing you
to "Yellow Submarine."

00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:28.868
-Yeah, so, when you're trying
to think about

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.734
what your first memory is, like,
you can't really know for sure.

00:04:30.734 --> 00:04:32.067
You're never holding a calendar.

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:35.267
So I have two nominees,
and they're both similar

00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:37.067
'cause I'm in the doorway
watching my mom,

00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:38.868
and she's sitting
in both options.

00:04:38.868 --> 00:04:42.133
The one, yes, lovely,
she's watching a cartoon.

00:04:42.133 --> 00:04:44.234
It's late at night.
I can't sleep.

00:04:44.234 --> 00:04:46.100
And she sits me down,
says, "Nana, come here."

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.767
My nickname was Nana.
I couldn't say "Brendan."

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:50.167
I said "Nana."
-Yeah.

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:51.601
-And she's like,
"This is 'Yellow Submarine.'"

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:53.467
And I have a specific memory
of John Lennon,

00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:55.334
like, climbing the tower
of the lyrics

00:04:55.334 --> 00:04:57.400
of "All You Need Is Love" that
he was making with his mouth.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.801
And I was like,
"That's really cool."

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:02.868
And history will show such
sentimental displays of love

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:04.300
not really my mom's wheelhouse,

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.033
because the other memory that
might be my first memory is,

00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:09.567
again, I'm standing in
the doorway, and she's sitting,

00:05:09.567 --> 00:05:12.501
but in this scenario,
she's sitting on the toilet

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:14.000
and she has her head
in her hands,

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.400
as she so often did,
and she's holding a cigarette,

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.667
and she sees me
and she sees the cigarette.

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:21.567
She goes, "What,
you want to try it?"

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:23.901
Like, "Sure."

00:05:23.901 --> 00:05:26.100
So I smoked a cigarette
at 3 years old,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.968
coughed my lungs out, and I've
never wanted a cigarette since.

00:05:28.968 --> 00:05:31.467
Unorthodox but effective.

00:05:31.467 --> 00:05:35.234
[ Laughter, applause ]

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:36.868
-When she went to the doctor

00:05:36.868 --> 00:05:38.367
and was like,
"Why can't he say 'Brendan'?"

00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:40.701
He's like, "I don't know.
Is he smoking?

00:05:40.701 --> 00:05:42.567
When did he have
his first cigarette?"

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:43.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:46.100
Hey, so we met at Boom Chicago,

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.400
and that's a comedy theater
in Amsterdam,

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:49.934
and they wrote a wonderful book

00:05:49.934 --> 00:05:52.400
about the 30 most important
years in Dutch history.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.400
It's very tongue-in-cheek.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:54.501
It's really just a book about

00:05:54.501 --> 00:05:56.567
this great theater
we were part of.

00:05:56.567 --> 00:06:00.734
And it got to add a chapter
about the last five years

00:06:00.734 --> 00:06:03.367
of what their alumni
have accomplished.

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:06.000
And, of course, you and
Jason Sudeikis and Joe Kelly --

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.868
there's a SAG strike,
so we can't get into it --

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:09.801
but you guys
wrote a very special show.

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:11.634
-Some show.
-Some show.

00:06:11.634 --> 00:06:13.834
But, you know, #Believe.
It was very good.

00:06:13.834 --> 00:06:17.767
And it's very cool to also hear
how your time in Amsterdam

00:06:17.767 --> 00:06:20.133
informed that incredible
piece of writing.

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:21.667
-Yeah, it was nice of them
to do that extra,

00:06:21.667 --> 00:06:24.300
'cause there was a 25-year
anniversary edition of the book.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.567
It was nice of them
to do this extra book.

00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:26.868
Suffice to say,

00:06:26.868 --> 00:06:28.734
I was not on the cover
of the 25th-anniversary edition.

00:06:28.734 --> 00:06:30.901
-Yeah, that's right.
That's the thing.

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:32.234
You were like, "You know what?

00:06:32.234 --> 00:06:35.267
I'm gonna go out and make
some cover-worthy work."

00:06:35.267 --> 00:06:37.367
Last thing, because again,
you've had a good five years.

00:06:37.367 --> 00:06:39.434
You threw out the first pitch
at a Chicago White Sox game.

00:06:39.434 --> 00:06:41.534
-Mm-hmm.
-It's not the worst picture.

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:43.400
You look like you might
be doing alright here.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.067
-I did okay. I did okay.
-You did okay?

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:47.434
-The only goal
was not become a meme.

00:06:47.434 --> 00:06:49.067
And it worked out.
-Yeah. That's the thing.

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:51.334
If you throw out a first pitch
and no one ever talks about it,

00:06:51.334 --> 00:06:53.100
that's all you ever want,

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.968
'cause the only ones they talk
about are the real bad ones.

00:06:55.968 --> 00:06:57.667
-Yeah. Please, just forget
about it completely.

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.267
-How much practice?

00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:01.767
-With Joe Kelly,
the aforementioned Joe Kelly,

00:07:01.767 --> 00:07:04.434
we went and threw
like three or four times.

00:07:04.434 --> 00:07:06.501
Joe Kelly, former catcher.
-Okay. So that helps.

00:07:06.501 --> 00:07:09.267
-But the practices
tended to be very short

00:07:09.267 --> 00:07:11.000
because I'm old and I was like,

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.467
"Alright, whoo.
Alright, here we go.

00:07:13.467 --> 00:07:15.601
Oh, Joe, I think that's
about enough for today.

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:16.934
Ohhh!"

00:07:16.934 --> 00:07:17.968
-Well, it worked out.

00:07:17.968 --> 00:07:19.701
And I'm very excited
for your show.

00:07:19.701 --> 00:07:20.801
And it's so great
to have you here, buddy.

00:07:20.801 --> 00:07:23.033
Thank you so much.
Brendan Hunt, everybody.

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:24.400
"The Movement You Need"
is playing

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.801
at the SoHo Playhouse
through November 7th.

00:07:26.801 --> 00:07:28.300
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

